# Expectation record for 13_floating_point/001_float_double_arithmetic.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 001_float_double_arithmetic
area               = 13_floating_point
description        = Single- and double-precision arithmetic at fixed print precision, folded and volatile runtime variants of every operator: add, subtract, multiply, divide, negate, a parenthesized chain, compound assignment, a call boundary, widening, narrowing and an excess-precision probe
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour by construction. The argument below is made for
what this program ACTUALLY does rather than for a stricter shape it does not have, because
undefined behaviour would permit either compiler to do anything and would leave a divergence
proving nothing; freedom from it is the precondition under which a divergence is evidence
about a compiler at all.

EXACTNESS IS THE LOAD-BEARING PROPERTY, so it is argued first. Every floating operand here is
a DYADIC RATIONAL -- m / 2^k with a small m and a small k, drawn only from 0.125, 0.25, 0.5,
0.75, 1.25, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 4.0, 8.0 and 10.0 -- and every intermediate and every final
result is one too. The widest result is 48.75, which is 110000.11 in binary and so needs eight
significand bits; the largest magnitude reached anywhere is 50.0, an intermediate of the double
chain. Eight bits fits binary32's twenty-four and binary64's fifty-three with room to spare, so
every value in this program is EXACTLY REPRESENTABLE IN BOTH float AND double and NO OPERATION
EVER MAKES A ROUNDING DECISION. Every float -> double widening and double -> float narrowing is
therefore lossless in both directions, which is also what lets the two agreement lines compare
a widened float against a double at all.
BECAUSE NOTHING ROUNDS, i686's EXCESS INTERMEDIATE PRECISION CANNOT CHANGE ANY RESULT -- the
specific hazard this program is written to defend against. i686 evaluates floating expressions
in x87 extended precision, FLT_EVAL_METHOD 2, while x86-64 uses SSE, AArch64 its FP/SIMD unit
and RISC-V 64 the F and D extensions. Excess precision perturbs a result only where a rounding
decision was taken, and here none ever is: an exact value computed in sixty-four significand
bits and stored back to twenty-four or fifty-three is the same value. A single operand that
needed rounding -- 0.1, or a quotient by anything other than a power of two -- would let i686
double-round and would manufacture a divergence that says nothing about any compiler.
NO NON-FINITE VALUE AND NO SIGNED ZERO is produced or relied upon: no not-a-number, no
infinity, no negative zero and no subnormal appears, and none is reachable. Every divisor is a
named non-zero power of two -- 2.0 and 4.0 in both precisions -- so there is no division by
zero; the smallest magnitude used anywhere is 0.125 and the largest is 50.0, so nothing
overflows and nothing decays toward the subnormal range; and no value is ever subtracted from
itself, so no zero of either sign arises. No value is compared against itself either, so no
self-inequality identity is assumed.

The remaining rulebook items, each argued for this program:
- NO SIGNED INTEGER OVERFLOW. Exactly three integer objects exist and each holds 0 or 1: the
  two cross-precision agreement results, and their conjunction with the twenty-one remaining
  folded-against-runtime equalities. No integer arithmetic other than that conjunction occurs.
- NO SHIFT OPERATOR appears anywhere, so no shift count can be out of range.
- NO CONVERSION TO OR FROM AN INTEGER TYPE OCCURS AT ALL, so the one undefined case of a
  floating-to-integer conversion -- an integral part not representable in the destination,
  C11 6.3.1.4p1 -- cannot arise here, and neither can an inexact integer-to-floating
  conversion. The three integer values are the RESULT TYPES of == and &&, which are int by
  definition rather than by conversion. General conversion boundaries in both directions are
  the subject of 002_float_int_conversions.c in this area.
- NO ALIASING VIOLATION AND NO DEPENDENCE ON REPRESENTATION. No floating object is read
  through a pointer of another type, punned through a union, or inspected byte-wise. The only
  pointers in the program are the `const char *` that each format-string literal decays to in
  its printf call; no pointer arithmetic is performed on one, no array is indexed, and no
  pointer is dereferenced by this translation unit at all, so no padding byte and no relative
  address of unrelated objects can be observed.
- NO READ OF UNINITIALIZED STORAGE. Every volatile operand is initialized in its own
  declaration, and every result object is either initialized in its declaration or assigned
  before its only read.
- NO OBJECT IS MODIFIED TWICE BETWEEN SEQUENCE POINTS. The four compound assignments of each
  FP_COMPOUND instantiation are four full expressions in statements of their own, and the
  macro is a do/while(0) so that one instantiation plus its semicolon is exactly one
  statement.
- AT MOST ONE SIDE-EFFECTING ARGUMENT PER CALL, and in fact none. No call in this program --
  neither printf nor either fp_scale helper -- receives an argument with a side effect: each
  helper argument is read into a plain local in a statement of its own first, and every printf
  argument is a plain local already holding a computed result.
- NOTHING DEPENDS ON UNSPECIFIED EVALUATION ORDER. Every macro parameter appears exactly once
  in every macro body, so no operand is evaluated twice; where a computation needs one value in
  two places, two DISTINCT volatile objects supply it -- vf_b / vf_b_alt and vd_b / vd_b_alt --
  rather than one object read twice. The runtime spelling does pair two volatile reads inside a
  single arithmetic expression: the two operands of a binary operator, not the arguments of a
  call, and the relative order of those two reads is indeed unspecified. No printed value can
  turn on it. The two objects are distinct, neither read modifies anything, no other side effect
  is interleaved between them, and the operator's value is the same whichever operand is fetched
  first, so both permitted orders produce the same stdout -- which is the property the oracle
  needs. Pairing them is also what forces the backend to emit a genuine two-operand instruction,
  which is the point of the runtime variant.

Print discipline, because the golden record below is compared byte for byte:
- EVERY FLOATING VALUE IS PRINTED WITH AN EXPLICIT .6 PRECISION. Each needs at most three
  fractional digits -- .750, .375, .125 and .625 are the widest -- so every decimal expansion
  terminates well inside the six digits printed, with three digits to spare. That margin is why
  no printed line can turn on a last-digit rounding decision, and it is why no default precision
  and no rounding mode is relied upon anywhere. The only other conversion specification used is
  %d, for the three integer flags, and no length modifier appears in either.
- EVERY CONVERSION IS WRITTEN WITH AN EXPLICIT CAST -- twenty-five (double) casts and two
  (float) casts, including the default argument promotion of every float handed to the variadic
  printf -- so the -Wconversion and -Wsign-conversion members of the gate are satisfied by
  construction rather than by luck. Area 13 sanctions no deviation from the default gate, so
  this record omits ub_audit_flags entirely and thereby accepts
  -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror in full.
- NO HEADER IS INCLUDED. bcc ships no stdio.h -- its bundled set is the nine required
  freestanding headers plus a bonus stdatomic.h -- so naming one would fail against bcc while
  succeeding against the reference compiler, a divergence caused by the test rather than by the
  compiler. printf is hand-declared as `int printf(const char *, ...);` and is the only library
  function used, so the artifact links against libc alone under -static. float.h is bundled but
  is deliberately not named either: no limit and no epsilon is consulted here, and the bundled
  set is the subject of 12_preprocessor/003_bundled_header_inclusion.c.
- main is `int main(void)` and returns 0, inside the 0..125 range this suite permits, so the
  recorded expect_exit names a status the operating system delivers rather than one it truncates.

HERMETIC, as constraint C4 requires: every input is a literal in the source. Nothing is opened,
no socket is created, no file is read or written, no environment variable and no program argument
is consulted, no heap storage is obtained, no clock is read and no randomness is drawn -- so a
cell's stdout is a function of the program and the compiler alone.

Verified rather than asserted: the default seven-flag warning gate named above produces ZERO
diagnostics in the single native compile-only invocation the suite makes of it, and
-fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all builds and RUNS TO COMPLETION in its one
instrumented native build, with empty stderr, exit status 0, and stdout byte-identical to the golden
record below. Both gates drive the REFERENCE COMPILER
alone and are never applied to the compiler under test, so the shared-flag discipline the
differential oracle rests on is untouched by them.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
THIS RECORD NARROWS NOTHING: four targets, three optimization levels, all three oracles, and no
expected-divergence marker. The field is written all the same, for two reasons. It records the
normalizations that keep ONE golden record valid on EVERY target, and the set of hazards
deliberately kept out of the program is as worth reading as the properties the program asserts.
And a divergence here is a GENUINE FINDING rather than an implementation-defined difference,
which is a claim that ought to be inspectable rather than implicit.

IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED PROPERTIES KEPT OUT OF THE OBSERVABLE OUTPUT, each measured across the
four targets rather than assumed:
- PLAIN CHAR SIGNEDNESS was measured as signed on x86_64 and i686 and UNSIGNED on aarch64 and
  riscv64. No plain char value is printed, and no char object appears in the program at all, so
  the difference cannot reach a printed line.
- LONG AND POINTER WIDTH were measured as 4 bytes on i686 and 8 bytes on the other three
  (docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457-462). No long, no size_t, no ptrdiff_t, no
  intptr_t and no pointer value is printed, and no length modifier appears in any conversion
  specification: the only integer destination anywhere is int, the same width on all four
  targets, and the only specifications used are %.6f and %d.
- NO ADDRESS OR POINTER VALUE IS PRINTED. The only pointers that exist are the format-string
  literals passed to printf, and %p appears in no conversion specification, so no address, no
  pointer difference and no alignment residue reaches standard output.
- LONG DOUBLE IS ABSENT ENTIRELY. Its representation splits the four targets into two camps of
  two, and the split is by FORMAT rather than by size: the x87 80-bit extended format on
  x86_64 AND i686 alike, padded to 16 bytes on the first and 12 on the second, against IEEE
  binary128 on aarch64 and riscv64. Measured with gcc 13.4.0: sizeof(long double) is 16, 12, 16
  and 16 across x86_64, i686, aarch64 and riscv64, while __LDBL_MANT_DIG__ is 64, 64, 113 and
  113 -- so the two x86 targets differ from each other only in storage padding and agree on the
  significand, and it is the 64-against-113 difference that makes the value comparison
  meaningless. The split is worth stating precisely, because describing it by size -- x87 on
  i686 alone, binary128 everywhere else -- misdescribes x86_64, which is x87 in 16 bytes. That
  is the one property in this area which
  genuinely cannot be compared for value across backends. The type is NOT dropped on that
  account, which constraint C3 would forbid: it is the subject of
  004_long_double_target_restricted.c in this area, the only record in the corpus permitted to
  narrow a comparison because of it. None of it leaks in here, so this program keeps the full
  four-way comparison.
- FLOATING EVALUATION METHOD differs -- i686 evaluates in x87 extended precision,
  FLT_EVAL_METHOD 2, while the other three do not -- and is neutralized by exactness rather
  than avoided. The argument sits in ub_notes above, because it is what makes the program free
  of a dependence on unspecified intermediate precision rather than merely tidy.
- INTEGER WIDTHS AND SIGNS ARE NOT AT ISSUE. The three printed integers are int flags holding 0
  or 1, so no width, no signedness and no representation choice can alter their rendering.

THE TWO-VARIANT RULE, which is why this program prints twice as many lines as it has properties.
Every computation is written once as a macro and instantiated twice: over literal constants,
which the folder evaluates at translation time, and over volatile-qualified operands, which must
be re-read from memory at run time and therefore cannot be folded. Each pair is printed on
adjacent fold_X / rt_X lines, and a final all_pairs_agree line asserts that the two spellings
agreed on every property at once -- which catches a pair agreeing with each other but with
neither expectation, something two separately wrong lines could otherwise be misread as. Without
the runtime half, -O1 and -O2 would answer every line with the folder's value, the
floating-point backend would never be asked to compute anything, and a code-generation defect
would escape while the suite reported PASS. With both halves present, a divergence localizes to
the folder or to the backend rather than to one of the two.

WHY ORACLE (b) IS ENABLED HERE, the claim most worth checking. Because every printed digit is
exact on every target, cross-backend value equality is meaningful for this program rather than a
comparison of legitimately different numbers. That is measured, not assumed: stdout is
byte-identical -- all 946 bytes of it -- with exit status 0, in all twelve
target-and-optimization-level cells.

PROVENANCE OF THE GOLDEN RECORD, which is what keeps oracle (c) independent. Every value below
was derived BY HAND from the C semantics of the program -- exact dyadic arithmetic, so the
derivation is unambiguous -- and then confirmed against the REFERENCE COMPILER in each of those
twelve cells. No compiler under test contributed to it. Seeding it from bcc would make oracle (c)
certify bcc against itself and would launder a miscompilation into the expectation, destroying
the one oracle that can detect both compilers changing behaviour in the same direction at the
same time, which oracle (a) reports as agreement. Regeneration is a maintenance action performed
only through tests/conformance/tools/regenerate_expected.sh and never automatically during a test
run, so a wrong answer cannot quietly become the new expectation.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
